<title>USA: Structural reform getting harder in C.Europe -- IMF.</title>
<headline>Structural reform getting harder in C.Europe -- IMF.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Most countries of central and eastern Europe have done well to stabilize and restructure their economies, but restructuring is becoming more difficult, the International Monetary Fund said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The IMF's World Economic Outlook said annual inflation had fallen below 20 percent in the states it described as &quot;more advanced in the transition process&quot;. All these countries' economies were growing.</p>
<p>The nine more advanced states were Croatia, Czech Republic, Estonia, Hungary, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Slovenia and the Slovak republic.  </p>
<p>&quot;Progress in structural reforms continue in these countries, although often at a slower pace than previously, reflecting the relatively difficult tasks now being addressed,&quot; the report said.</p>
<p>These tasks included restructuring enterprises, rebuilding infrastructures and establishing robust financial and legal institutions, it added.</p>
<p>The IMF described Albania, Bulgaria, Macedonia and Romania, along with all 12 members of the Commonwealth of Independent States, as &quot;countries less advanced in transition&quot;.  </p>
<p>The IMF said high wage increases were fuelling domestic demand in the Czech and Slovak republics, raising the risk that the economies could overheat.</p>
<p>The Hungarian economy had slowed, but chances there remained good, the IMF added.</p>
<p>&quot;Tight fiscal and monetary policies have established important conditions for robust and sustainable growth (in Hungary); indeed signs of a pickup in activity emerged in the second half of 1996,&quot; the report said.</p>
